{
  "case_id": "2010-09-11556",
  "document_hash": "0dc813d5705db3669deb347315823e366d9330812018542ccd9e3c65edd21a0b",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2010-09-11556",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0dc813d5705db3669deb347315823e366d9330812018542ccd9e3c65edd21a0b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0dc813d5705d-0000-0000",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 1,
        "start": 1
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "KRISTINA SCHWARZ, ESQ., New York, New York; on behalf of Petitioner.\nCAITLIN J. HALLIGAN, ESQ., General Counsel, New York County District Attorney, New York, New York; on behalf of Respondent.\nPRATIK A. SHAH, ESQ., Assistant to the Solicitor General, Department of Justice, Washington, D.C.; on behalf of United States, as amicus curiae."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2010-09-11556",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0dc813d5705db3669deb347315823e366d9330812018542ccd9e3c65edd21a0b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0dc813d5705d-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: We will hear argument next this morning in Case 09-11556, Tolentino v. New York."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner (Kristina Schwarz)",
      "case_id": "2010-09-11556",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0dc813d5705db3669deb347315823e366d9330812018542ccd9e3c65edd21a0b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0dc813d5705d-0000-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The Court held in Delaware v. Prouse that, in the absence of reasonable suspicion, it is an unreasonable seizure under the Fourth Amendment for police to stop a car for the purpose of checking the motorist's driving credentials."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner (Kristina Schwarz)",
      "case_id": "2010-09-11556",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0dc813d5705db3669deb347315823e366d9330812018542ccd9e3c65edd21a0b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0dc813d5705d-0000-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Petitioner's motion to suppress alleged that the police acted exactly as they did in Prouse and Brown. They stopped the car without justification,"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2010-09-11556",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0dc813d5705db3669deb347315823e366d9330812018542ccd9e3c65edd21a0b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0dc813d5705d-0000-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Was -- was -- was the poisonous fruit the DMV records, or was the poisonous fruit the fact that this person who is contained in the records was the one driving the car?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2010-09-11556",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0dc813d5705db3669deb347315823e366d9330812018542ccd9e3c65edd21a0b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0dc813d5705d-0000-0006",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Suppose that -- that you had won at the trial level; the judge suppresses everything and dismisses the indictment; and Mr. Tolentino walks out of the courtroom and is observed by the officer that conducted the stop in this case. The officer sees him getting into a car and driving away, even though his license is still suspended. Now, could -- could he be arrested for that?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2010-09-11556",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0dc813d5705db3669deb347315823e366d9330812018542ccd9e3c65edd21a0b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0dc813d5705d-0000-0007",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 7,
        "start": 7
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Is there -- is there any case in this Court where what was suppressed was information that was lawfully in the government's possession, as opposed to evidence that was acquired originally through the search?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2010-09-11556",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0dc813d5705db3669deb347315823e366d9330812018542ccd9e3c65edd21a0b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0dc813d5705d-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BREYER: Yes, and I was testing that. I want to know: Is that, in your opinion, a correct rule in all cases?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Schwarz",
      "case_id": "2010-09-11556",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0dc813d5705db3669deb347315823e366d9330812018542ccd9e3c65edd21a0b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0dc813d5705d-0001-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. SCHWARZ: No. And in fact, one of the -\u00ad JUSTICE BREYER: I mean, not an incorrect rule, the opposite rule. No rule. MS. SCHWARZ: Well, it's -- the problem with that rule, the categorical rule, is that it will create a fresh incentive for police officers to make these kind of suspicionless stops, and so it will encourage police to violate the Fourth Amendment, and not only -\u00ad"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Halligan",
      "case_id": "2010-09-11556",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0dc813d5705db3669deb347315823e366d9330812018542ccd9e3c65edd21a0b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0dc813d5705d-0001-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. HALLIGAN: Mr. Chief Justice, and may it please the Court: I would like to start with your question, Justice Breyer, about what the ruling below was and what's at stake in this case. What the court of appeals held was quite narrow. It said -- and I'm reading from page 105a of the appendix: \"We merely hold that a defendant may not invoke the fruit of the poisonous tree doctrine when the only link between improper police activity and the disputed evidence is that the police learned the defendant's name.\""
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2010-09-11556",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0dc813d5705db3669deb347315823e366d9330812018542ccd9e3c65edd21a0b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0dc813d5705d-0001-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 30,
        "start": 30
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BREYER: He made a mistake and said: I'm driving the car."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Halligan",
      "case_id": "2010-09-11556",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0dc813d5705db3669deb347315823e366d9330812018542ccd9e3c65edd21a0b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0dc813d5705d-0001-0005",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 34,
        "start": 34
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. HALLIGAN: I think it's very different than when you have drugs in a car which could clearly be subject to suppression. All that you have here, first of all, that's elicited, is the name. The name itself is not subject to suppression, nor did the defendant seek to have it suppressed, as the court of appeals observed. And so that should be the end of the inquiry."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Halligan",
      "case_id": "2010-09-11556",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0dc813d5705db3669deb347315823e366d9330812018542ccd9e3c65edd21a0b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0dc813d5705d-0002-0000",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "previously, although it's not in the record, the DMV records, along with other records, such as arrest warrants, are in fact in the NYPD's possession. They use a database that the State police generate which they download onto their server. So they have it in their actual possession, which is different from -"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Prosecution",
      "case_id": "2010-09-11556",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0dc813d5705db3669deb347315823e366d9330812018542ccd9e3c65edd21a0b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0dc813d5705d-0002-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 39,
        "start": 39
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The prosecutor made two arguments on the -- in response to the suppression motion. One was that the DMV records were not subject to suppression as a category. The second was, as you say, that the stop was legal."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2010-09-11556",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0dc813d5705db3669deb347315823e366d9330812018542ccd9e3c65edd21a0b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0dc813d5705d-0002-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 39,
        "start": 39
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: May I ask you something about the practice in New York? I mean, there is an artificiality to this case because we are assuming that the stop was unlawful."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2010-09-11556",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0dc813d5705db3669deb347315823e366d9330812018542ccd9e3c65edd21a0b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0dc813d5705d-0002-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 41,
        "start": 41
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: Counsel, do you see no difference between Crews and Bynum, in the typical case where the evidence against the defendant is not developed as a result of an illegal stop -- it exists independent of that stop -- and one in which the stop itself creates the ground for arrest? Don't you see a difference between those two things?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2010-09-11556",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0dc813d5705db3669deb347315823e366d9330812018542ccd9e3c65edd21a0b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0dc813d5705d-0002-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 41,
        "start": 41
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KAGAN: Your government records argument, is it limited to New York City records or does it also apply to FBI records, to records of other cities and states which, presumably, would be available on a reciprocal basis?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Pratik A. Shah",
      "case_id": "2010-09-11556",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0dc813d5705db3669deb347315823e366d9330812018542ccd9e3c65edd21a0b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0dc813d5705d-0002-0002",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 43,
        "start": 43
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Petitioner seeks to suppress official records that were lawfully in the State's possession before any Fourth Amendment violation occurred. That unprecedented request should be rejected for three reasons. One, the DMV records were accessed merely through use of Petitioner's name; two, those records were produced and possessed by the State long before the allegedly illegal stop at issue; and, three, deterrence does not outweigh the costs, the substantial costs of suppression under the circumstances present here."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2010-09-11556",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0dc813d5705db3669deb347315823e366d9330812018542ccd9e3c65edd21a0b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0dc813d5705d-0002-0006",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 46,
        "start": 46
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BREYER: Why? The facts of this case, I find confusing. Let's imagine the policeman goes with a hatchet and breaks into somebody's house illegally."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2010-09-11556",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0dc813d5705db3669deb347315823e366d9330812018542ccd9e3c65edd21a0b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0dc813d5705d-0003-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 56,
        "start": 56
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Why? Because when he entered a guilty plea, you reserved only the question concerning the motor vehicle records; you didn't reserve any other questions."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Schwarz",
      "case_id": "2010-09-11556",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0dc813d5705db3669deb347315823e366d9330812018542ccd9e3c65edd21a0b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0dc813d5705d-0003-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 56,
        "start": 56
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Well, but at the suppression hearing, the -- the court would be free to consider all of the suppressible fruit, including the -- the statement that was made and the observations and also the DMV records."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2010-09-11556",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0dc813d5705db3669deb347315823e366d9330812018542ccd9e3c65edd21a0b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0dc813d5705d-0003-0002",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 56,
        "start": 56
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The case is submitted."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Source extraction",
      "case_id": "2010-09-11556",
      "confidence": 0.0,
